from . import n
n("cell_types","Tissues and cell differentiation","6",(1e-5,1e-2),"C1","Biology",
 "Humans have about 200 cell types arranged in four tissue kinds: epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous. They differ in which genes are active, not in their DNA.",
 "Stem cells can renew themselves and give rise to specialised cells. Induced pluripotent stem cells show that adult cells can be reprogrammed.",
 "≈37×10¹² cells in a human; iPSC via Oct4, Sox2, Klf4, c-Myc (Yamanaka 2006).",
 "Cell-type counts depend on the classification scheme.",
 em="gene_regulation cell_biology_intro",ev="Single-cell RNA sequencing|Nuclear transfer",refs="ANP 4; BIO 33",ex="development_biology",sim="tissue")
n("development_biology","Embryonic development","7",(1e-5,1),"C1","Developmental biology",
 "A fertilised egg divides and organises into a body through gradients of signalling molecules. Hox genes mark positions along the head-to-tail axis.",
 "Cells decide fates by reading morphogen concentrations and neighbours. Regeneration and organoid research try to reuse these programmes.",
 "Human gestation ≈266 days; ≈10¹⁰ divisions; Bicoid gradient length ≈100 μm in fly embryo; Turing reaction-diffusion patterns.",
 "How genomes encode 3D form is incompletely understood.",
 em="gene_regulation cell_types",ev="Fate maps|Hox mutants",refs="BIO 43; ANP 28",ex="aging_biology",fr="Whether ageing can be substantially slowed~open")
n("aging_biology","Ageing","7",(1e-9,2),"C3","Biology",
 "Bodies gradually lose function as damage accumulates and repair slows. Hallmarks include DNA damage, shortened telomeres and worn-out cells.",
 "Calorie restriction extends lifespan in many animals, and some drugs lengthen it in mice. Applying this to humans is unproven.",
 "Telomere loss ≈50–100 bp per division; Hayflick limit ≈50 divisions; human max lifespan ≈122 y.",
 "Mechanistic theories compete.",
 em="development_biology cell_cycle",ev="Progeria studies|Longevity across species",refs="ANP 28",fr="Mechanisms and modifiability of human ageing~open")
n("homeostasis","Homeostasis and the human body plan","7",(1e-2,3),"C1","Physiology",
 "Body systems cooperate to keep temperature, blood sugar and acidity near set points using negative feedback. Examples include shivering, sweating and insulin release.",
 "Hormones and nerves act as sensors, controllers and effectors. Failure of these loops leads to diseases like diabetes.",
 "Core temp 37 °C; blood glucose ≈5 mmol/L fasting; blood pH 7.35–7.45; HR ≈70 bpm.",
 "Set points shift with age, circadian rhythm and stress.",
 em="cell_signaling cell_types",ev="Claude Bernard, Cannon",refs="ANP 1; BIO 33",ex="blood_circulation respiratory_physiology endocrine_physiology",sim="human")
n("blood_circulation","Heart and circulation","7",(1e-3,1),"C1","Physiology",
 "The heart pumps blood through arteries, tiny capillaries and veins, delivering oxygen and removing wastes. It beats about 100,000 times a day.",
 "Pressure from the left ventricle drives flow; valves keep it one way. The heart's electrical pacemaker is the sinoatrial node.",
 "Cardiac output ≈5 L/min at rest, stroke volume ≈70 mL; BP 120/80 mmHg; capillary diameter ≈8 μm; aorta ≈2.5 cm.",
 "Poiseuille flow Q=πr⁴ΔP/8ηL for laminar flow.",
 em="navier_stokes homeostasis action_potential",ev="Pressure catheters|Echocardiography",refs="ANP 19; ANP 20; BIO 40",sim="human tissue")
n("respiratory_physiology","Lungs and gas exchange","7",(1e-6,1e-1),"C1","Physiology",
 "Lungs bring oxygen into the blood and remove carbon dioxide across thin membranes of about 500 million tiny air sacs. Haemoglobin carries oxygen.",
 "Breathing is driven by the diaphragm creating lower pressure in the chest. Gas moves across by diffusion.",
 "Alveolar area ≈70 m²; membrane thickness ≈0.5 μm; tidal volume ≈0.5 L; 12–16 breaths/min; SaO₂ ≈97–99 %.",
 "Altitude reduces partial pressure of O₂.",
 em="homeostasis heat_diffusion heme_oxygen",ev="Spirometry|Blood-gas analysis",refs="ANP 22; BIO 39",sim="human air")
n("heme_oxygen","Haemoglobin and oxygen transport","5",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Biochemistry",
 "Haemoglobin in red blood cells holds iron-containing heme groups that bind oxygen in the lungs and release it in tissues. Carbon monoxide binds more strongly and is poisonous.",
 "The four subunits bind oxygen cooperatively, giving an S-shaped saturation curve that suits loading and unloading.",
 "Hb: 4 subunits, 4 heme-Fe; ≈1.34 mL O₂/g Hb; P₅₀≈26 mmHg; Hill n≈2.8; red cell ≈7 μm.",
 "Shifts with pH, CO₂ and 2,3-BPG.",
 em="inorganic_coordination protein_structure",ev="Perutz structure 1959",refs="ANP 18; BIO 39",sim="protein molecule")
n("digestion_renal","Digestion and kidneys","7",(1e-6,1e1),"C1","Physiology",
 "The gut breaks food into small molecules with enzymes and acid, and the kidneys filter blood to remove wastes and regulate water and salts.",
 "Each kidney contains about a million nephrons. The large intestine and microbes recover water and make some vitamins.",
 "Gut length ≈7–9 m; stomach pH 1.5–3.5; GFR ≈125 mL/min; ≈180 L filtered, 1.5 L urine per day.",
 "Digestive times vary with diet.",
 em="homeostasis enzymes membrane_transport",ev="Clinical physiology",refs="ANP 23; ANP 25; BIO 34",sim="human")
n("endocrine_physiology","Hormones and the endocrine system","7",(1e-9,1),"C1","Physiology",
 "Glands release hormones into the blood to coordinate growth, metabolism, stress and reproduction. Insulin and glucagon keep blood sugar steady.",
 "Hormones act on receptors on or inside target cells. The hypothalamus and pituitary form the control centre.",
 "Insulin 5.8 kDa; cortisol peaks near waking; thyroxine regulates basal metabolic rate; feedback loops τ from minutes to days.",
 "Many interactions between hormones.",
 em="cell_signaling homeostasis",ev="Hormone assays|Gland removal experiments",refs="ANP 17; BIO 37",sim="human")
n("musculoskeletal","Bones, joints and muscle","7",(1e-3,1),"C1","Physiology",
 "Bones form a rigid lever system, and muscles pull on them to move the body. Bone is a living composite of mineral and collagen that remodels under load.",
 "Muscles are built of bundles of fibres that shorten when actin and myosin filaments slide past each other, driven by ATP and triggered by calcium.",
 "206 bones; femur bears several times body weight; sarcomere ≈2.5 μm; muscle stress ≈0.3 MPa.",
 "Bone density declines with age.",
 em="cytoskeleton_motors elasticity_materials",ev="Biomechanics|Electron microscopy of sarcomeres",refs="ANP 6; ANP 10; ANP 11",ex="muscle_contraction",sim="human")
n("muscle_contraction","Muscle contraction","6",(1e-9,1e-3),"C1","Physiology",
 "Muscle shortens when myosin heads grip actin filaments and pull, powered by ATP. A nerve signal releases calcium, which unlocks the filaments.",
 "Each myosin head cycle moves actin about 5–10 nm and the ratchet is repeated thousands of times per second across a fibre.",
 "Sarcomere shortening ≈ 1 μm; force per myosin head ≈3–5 pN; Ca²⁺ ≈10⁻⁷→10⁻⁵ M triggers contraction.",
 "Cross-bridge model; details of power stroke debated.",
 em="cytoskeleton_motors action_potential",ev="X-ray fibre diffraction (Huxley)|Optical trap",refs="ANP 10; BIO 38",sim="tissue protein")
n("skin_integument","Skin and the body's barrier","7",(1e-6,1e-2),"C1","Physiology",
 "Skin is the body's largest organ, forming a waterproof barrier that regulates temperature and senses touch. Its outer layer is made of dead keratin-filled cells that constantly shed.",
 "Melanin absorbs UV; sweat glands cool by evaporation. Skin renews roughly every month.",
 "Area ≈1.7–2 m²; epidermis thickness 0.05–1.5 mm; sweat evaporation ≈2.4 MJ/kg.",
 "Thickness and pigment vary across body and individuals.",
 em="cell_types homeostasis",ev="Dermatology",refs="ANP 5",sim="skin")
n("immune_system","Immune system: innate and adaptive","7",(1e-9,1e-2),"C1","Immunology",
 "The immune system defends against pathogens with barriers, fast generic responses (innate) and slower learned ones (adaptive) that remember past infections.",
 "B cells make antibodies and T cells coordinate or kill infected cells. Gene shuffling creates an enormous variety of receptors.",
 "V(D)J recombination ≈10¹¹–10¹⁵ possible receptors; antibody ≈150 kDa; fever ≈38–39 °C; memory lasts years to decades.",
 "Autoimmunity and allergy are failures of discrimination.",
 em="cell_signaling cell_types",ev="Vaccination|Immunodeficiency studies",refs="BIO 42; MIC 17; MIC 18; ANP 21",ex="vaccines_medicine",fr="Triggers of autoimmunity~open|Immunotherapy mechanisms~open",sim="cell:animal")
n("vaccines_medicine","Germ theory, vaccines and antibiotics","7",(1e-9,1),"C1","Medicine",
 "Infectious diseases are caused by microbes. Sanitation, vaccines and antibiotics have produced the largest gains in life expectancy in history.",
 "Vaccines prime memory immunity without disease; antibiotics kill bacteria or stop their growth. Resistance evolves by natural selection.",
 "Smallpox eradicated 1980; penicillin 1928/1940s; R₀ measles ≈12–18; herd immunity threshold 1−1/R₀.",
 "Antibiotic resistance erodes treatments.",
 em="immune_system bacteria_archaea natural_selection",ev="Koch's postulates|Eradication of smallpox",refs="MIC 14; MIC 16; MIC 13",ex="pharmacology",fr="Antimicrobial resistance trajectories~open")
n("pharmacology","Pharmacology and drug action","7",(1e-10,1e-2),"C1","Medicine",
 "Most drugs work by binding to specific proteins like receptors or enzymes and altering their activity. Dose determines effect and side effects.",
 "Drug development moves from molecule discovery to randomised controlled trials, the gold standard for showing a treatment works.",
 "Dose-response E=E_max C/(EC₅₀+C); half-life t_{1/2}=0.693V_d/CL; IC₅₀ nM–μM; aspirin inhibits COX.",
 "Individual response varies with genes and other drugs.",
 em="organic_functional_groups protein_structure enzymes",ev="Clinical trials",refs="ORG 29",sim="molecule")
n("cancer_biology","Cancer","7",(1e-6,1e-1),"C1","Medicine",
 "Cancer arises when accumulated mutations let cells ignore growth controls and spread. It is many diseases, not one.",
 "Mutations in oncogenes and tumour-suppressor genes such as p53 combine over years. Immunotherapy releases immune cells against tumours.",
 "Typically 2–8 driver mutations; ≈10¹² cells in a 1 cm³ tumour; p53 mutated in ≈50 % of cancers.",
 "Risk rises steeply with age.",
 em="cell_cycle cell_signaling dna_structure",ev="Cancer genome sequencing|Epidemiology",refs="BIO 10; BIO 9",fr="Immunotherapy resistance~open",sim="cell:animal")
n("medical_imaging","Medical imaging","7",(1e-4,1),"C1","Medicine",
 "X-rays, CT, MRI, ultrasound and PET let doctors see inside the body. Each uses a different physical interaction: absorption, nuclear spin, echoes or positron annihilation.",
 "MRI uses strong magnets and radio pulses on hydrogen nuclei in water; PET uses antimatter, since positrons annihilate with electrons.",
 "MRI Larmor f=42.58 MHz/T (1.5–3 T); X-ray 20–150 keV; ultrasound 2–15 MHz; PET γ=511 keV.",
 "Ionising radiation has dose limits.",
 em="electromagnetic_waves spectroscopy_methods antimatter_asymmetry",ev="Clinical use",refs="UP3 10; ANP 1",sim="human")
n("mri_tech","Magnetic resonance imaging","7",(1e-4,1),"C1","Technology",
 "MRI aligns the spins of hydrogen nuclei with a strong magnetic field, nudges them with radio pulses, and listens to the signals they emit as they relax.",
 "Superconducting magnets give the field. Contrast comes from differences in how fast different tissues relax.",
 "ω_L=γB₀, γ/2π=42.58 MHz/T; T₁≈1 s, T₂≈50–100 ms in tissue.",
 "Contraindicated with some metal implants.",
 em="spectroscopy_methods superconductivity",ev="Clinical use since 1980s",refs="UP3 10")
# ---- nervous system ----
n("action_potential","Neurons and action potentials","6",(1e-8,1),"C1","Neuroscience",
 "Neurons send electrical pulses called action potentials along their fibres. The pulse is produced by sodium and potassium ions flowing through channels in the membrane.",
 "The Hodgkin-Huxley model describes how voltage-gated channels open and close in sequence. Signals jump between myelin gaps to travel faster.",
 "Resting V≈−70 mV; threshold ≈−55 mV; peak ≈+40 mV; conduction 1–120 m/s; C_m≈1 μF/cm²; C dV/dt=−Σg_i(V−E_i).",
 "Stochastic channel opening adds noise.",
 em="membrane_transport circuits_ohm",ev="Squid giant axon (Hodgkin-Huxley 1952)|Patch clamp",refs="ANP 12; BIO 35",ex="synapse_plasticity",sim="cell:animal membrane")
n("synapse_plasticity","Synapses and plasticity","6",(1e-9,1e-5),"C1","Neuroscience",
 "At a synapse one neuron releases chemicals (neurotransmitters) that excite or inhibit the next. Changing synapse strength with use is how brains learn.",
 "Long-term potentiation strengthens synapses after repeated activity. Dopamine, serotonin and others modulate circuits.",
 "Synaptic cleft ≈20 nm; vesicle ≈40 nm, ≈5,000 molecules ACh; ≈10¹⁴ synapses in a human brain; delay ≈0.5 ms.",
 "Mechanisms of memory storage across timescales are still debated.",
 em="action_potential membrane_structure",ev="Hippocampal LTP (Bliss and Lømo)|Pharmacology of neurotransmitters",refs="ANP 12; BIO 35; PSY 3",ex="brain_organization memory_learning",sim="cell:animal protein")
n("brain_organization","The brain: structure and function","7",(1e-3,2e-1),"C1","Neuroscience",
 "The human brain has about 86 billion neurons organised into regions for senses, movement, memory and reasoning. The cerebral cortex handles many higher functions.",
 "Brain regions specialise but work in networks. Imaging methods like fMRI show where activity changes, not the precise neural code.",
 "86×10⁹ neurons, ≈10¹⁴ synapses, mass ≈1.4 kg, power ≈20 W (≈20 % of body); cortex ≈2.5 mm thick.",
 "Localisation of function is approximate.",
 em="synapse_plasticity",ev="Lesion studies|fMRI|EEG",refs="ANP 13; PSY 3",ex="cognition_psychology consciousness_theories connectome",sim="human")
n("connectome","Connectomics and neural wiring","7",(1e-8,1e-2),"C2","Neuroscience",
 "Connectomics maps every neuron and synapse in a brain using electron microscopy. Complete maps exist for small animals and growing parts of mammal brains.",
 "Wiring maps provide constraints for circuit models, but they do not give the strengths, chemistry or dynamics by themselves.",
 "C. elegans: 302 neurons, ≈7,000 synapses; mouse cortex 1 mm³ ≈ 10⁵ neurons.",
 "Structure alone does not determine function.",
 em="brain_organization graph_theory",ev="Electron-microscopy reconstructions",refs="ANP 13",fref="flywire_connectome",sim="tissue")
n("sensory_systems","Senses: vision, hearing, touch, taste and smell","7",(1e-9,1),"C1","Neuroscience",
 "Sensory cells convert physical signals (light, pressure waves, molecules) into electrical signals the brain interprets.",
 "The retina has about 92 million rods and 4.6 million cones (Curcio et al. 1990, eight retinas; individuals vary by about 15 %); hair cells in the cochlea sense vibrations; olfactory receptors detect molecules.",
 "Human vision 380–750 nm; hearing 20 Hz–20 kHz; ≈400 olfactory receptor genes; a rod can respond to a single photon.",
 "Perception is a constructive process, not a recording.",
 em="action_potential electromagnetic_waves waves",ev="Psychophysics|Electrophysiology",refs="BIO 36; PSY 5",ex="vision_eye sound_hearing",sim="human")
n("vision_eye","How the eye forms images","7",(1e-6,3e-2),"C1","Neuroscience",
 "The cornea and lens focus light onto the retina, where photoreceptors convert it into nerve signals. The brain assembles these into what we see.",
 "Rhodopsin changes shape when it absorbs a photon, triggering a chemical cascade. Three cone types give colour vision.",
 "Eye focal length ≈17 mm (≈60 D); cone peaks ≈420, 534, 564 nm; retinal isomerisation 11-cis→all-trans.",
 "Optical limits set by pupil diffraction and cone spacing (~2 μm).",
 em="sensory_systems optics_geometric",ev="Ophthalmology|Retinal recordings",refs="BIO 36; PSY 5",sim="human")
n("sound_hearing","Sound and hearing","7",(1e-9,1e2),"C1","Neuroscience",
 "Sound is pressure waves in air; the ear converts them into nerve signals. Pitch depends on frequency and loudness on amplitude.",
 "Different places on the cochlear membrane resonate at different frequencies. Loudness is measured in decibels.",
 "dB=10 log(I/I₀), I₀=10⁻¹² W/m²; threshold ≈20 μPa; speech 0.3–3 kHz; cochlea length ≈35 mm.",
 "Hearing declines with age and noise exposure.",
 em="waves sensory_systems",ev="Audiometry",refs="UP1 17; PSY 5",sim="human air")
n("memory_learning","Memory and learning","7",(1e-3,1),"C2","Cognitive science",
 "Memory has working memory (a few items), and long-term memory consolidated during sleep. Learning links stimuli and rewards.",
 "Dopamine signals reward-prediction errors that drive reinforcement learning. Spaced practice improves retention.",
 "Working memory ≈4 chunks; Ebbinghaus forgetting curve R=e^{−t/S}; Rescorla-Wagner ΔV=αβ(λ−ΣV).",
 "Models describe typical behaviour; individual variation is large.",
 em="synapse_plasticity brain_organization",ev="Patient H.M.|Conditioning experiments",refs="PSY 8; PSY 6",ex="cognition_psychology")
n("cognition_psychology","Cognition, emotion and behaviour","7",(1e-3,1e2),"C2","Psychology",
 "Psychology studies perception, thinking, emotion and personality. Reliable findings include limited attention, heuristics and biases, and conditioning.",
 "Heritability of many traits is moderate (≈30–60 %), meaning genes and environment both matter.",
 "Twin studies h² for IQ ≈0.5–0.8 in adults; Kahneman-Tversky prospect theory; Big Five traits.",
 "Findings must be judged one by one; many classic effects failed replication.",
 em="brain_organization memory_learning",ev="Experimental psychology",refs="PSY 7; PSY 10; PSY 11; PSY 12",ex="replication_crisis")
n("replication_crisis","The replication crisis","7",None,"C1","Psychology",
 "Large replication projects found that many published psychology results shrink or disappear when repeated. Ego depletion and power posing are examples.",
 "Small samples, flexible analysis and publication bias inflate false positives. Pre-registration and larger studies are the main remedies.",
 "Open Science Collaboration 2015: ≈36 % of 100 replications significant vs 97 % original; effect size halved.",
 "Rates differ between fields and methods.",
 em="bayes_stats cognition_psychology",ev="Many-Labs replications",refs="PSY 2; IST 9",ex="scientific_method")
n("consciousness_theories","Consciousness","7",(1e-3,1),"C3","Neuroscience",
 "Why brain activity comes with subjective experience is unsolved. Rival theories include integrated information, global neuronal workspace, higher-order and predictive-processing views.",
 "Experiments compare brain activity for stimuli that are seen versus unseen. No consensus theory exists, and no test settles whether a machine could be conscious.",
 "Integrated information Φ (IIT); global workspace ignition ≈300 ms post-stimulus (P3b).",
 "Measurement problem: reports versus experience.",
 em="brain_organization",ev="Binocular rivalry|Anaesthesia studies|Blindsight",refs="PSY 4",fref="cogitate",fr="The 'hard problem' remains open~open")
n("predictive_brain","Predictive processing in the brain","7",(1e-3,1),"C3","Neuroscience",
 "One influential idea says the brain constantly predicts its inputs and learns from errors. It links perception, action and learning in one framework.",
 "The free-energy principle formalises this. Evidence is mixed and the idea is hard to falsify.",
 "Free energy F=E_q[ln q(s)−ln p(o,s)]; prediction error ε=o−ĝ(μ).",
 "Framework status, not confirmed mechanism.",
 em="brain_organization bayes_stats",ev="Mismatch negativity|Illusions",refs="PSY 5",fr="Neural implementation of predictions unresolved~open")
n("sleep_circadian","Sleep and circadian rhythms","7",(1e-3,1e5),"C2","Physiology",
 "Sleep follows a roughly 24-hour clock driven by cells in the brain and genes that cycle in expression. Its functions include memory consolidation and clearing metabolic waste.",
 "Light resets the clock via special retinal cells. Lack of sleep impairs attention and health.",
 "Circadian period ≈24.2 h; REM cycles ≈90 min; adults need ≈7–9 h; melatonin peaks at night.",
 "Exact functions of sleep are not fully known.",
 em="homeostasis brain_organization",ev="Sleep-deprivation studies|Clock gene mutants",refs="PSY 4",fr="Core function of sleep~open")
# ---- ecology ----
n("ecology_energy","Ecosystems and energy flow","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Ecology",
 "Ecosystems get energy from sunlight, and only about 10 % passes from each feeding level to the next. This limits how many top predators can exist.",
 "Producers capture light, consumers eat them, and decomposers recycle nutrients. Food webs link species in complex networks.",
 "Transfer efficiency ≈10 % (range 5–20 %); global NPP ≈105 Pg C/yr (land ≈55, ocean ≈50); only ≈1 % of sunlight is fixed by plants.",
 "Efficiency varies with ecosystem.",
 em="photosynthesis_light second_law graph_theory",ev="Trophic-level biomass pyramids",refs="BIO 46; COB 20",ex="biogeochemical_cycles",sim="pond park")
n("population_dynamics","Population dynamics and species interactions","8",(1,1e7),"C1","Ecology",
 "Populations grow until limited by food or space. Predators and prey, competitors and partners shape each other's numbers.",
 "The logistic equation and Lotka-Volterra models capture basic cycles. Mutualism, parasitism and competition appear throughout nature.",
 "dN/dt=rN(1−N/K); Lotka-Volterra ẋ=αx−βxy, ẏ=δxy−γy; r-K strategies.",
 "Real populations show noise, age structure, and chaos in some cases.",
 em="ode_pde natural_selection dynamical_chaos",ev="Hare-lynx records|Lab microbe populations",refs="BIO 45; COB 19",ex="biodiversity_conservation",sim="pond park")
n("biodiversity_conservation","Biodiversity, extinction and conservation","8",(1,1e7),"C2","Ecology",
 "Species richness rises with area and falls with isolation (island biogeography). Current extinction rates are far above the long-term background.",
 "Habitat loss, overharvest, invasive species, pollution and climate change are the main pressures. Ecosystems provide services such as pollination and water filtering.",
 "Species-area S=cA^z, z≈0.25; background extinction ≈0.1–1 per million species-years.",
 "Rates for poorly known groups are uncertain.",
 em="population_dynamics speciation_biodiversity",ev="IUCN Red List|Island studies",refs="BIO 47; COB 21",fr="Predicting ecosystem response to warming and extinctions~open|Ecological tipping points~open",sim="park bee tree")
n("biosphere_energy","Life reshaping Earth: oxygenation","8",(1e3,1e7),"C1","Earth system",
 "Early photosynthesising bacteria filled the air with oxygen around 2.4 billion years ago, the Great Oxidation Event, transforming chemistry and enabling later complex life.",
 "Banded iron formations record the oxygen sink. Later, plants on land changed erosion and the carbon cycle.",
 "O₂ rose from <10⁻⁵ to ≈1–10 % of present level around 2.4–2.1 Ga; present 21 %.",
 "Timing and rate of oxygenation debated in detail.",
 em="photosynthesis_light deep_time",ev="Banded iron formations|Sulfur isotope signals",refs="BIO 8",ex="gas_air_properties")
